@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — a protocol on the down that does not stir
#
# Taken up because the sign is external. Most of what this graph holds about
# breathing is reported from inside: a warmth, a lightness, a stillness that
# only the practitioner can attest. Here a second person, standing beside the
# practitioner, sees the same thing he does, and the material and the place
# are named.
#

pmp:FeatherAtTheNostrilExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the down at nose and mouth stays still"@en, "Ob der Flaum an Nase und Mund stillsteht"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the mark the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi sets on the closing off of the breath: a goose down is laid on the nose and the mouth, and that it does not stir while the qi is let out is the sign. The passage gives this as the criterion by which the practitioner knows he is doing the thing, alongside the rule that he is not to hear his own breathing and that he is always to take in more than he gives out."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:InaudibleAndUnevenBreath ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A practitioner who claims the attainment and whose down stirs on the exhalation, observed by someone other than himself. The source states the sign without qualification and attaches no condition of hour, place or state to it, so a stirring falls against the claim as the chapter makes it. The converse does not establish the doctrine: a down that lies still shows a slow exhalation and nothing about the qi." ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, working from the passage recorded in traditions/daoist.ttl" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-09" ;
    skos:note "The state is procedure-without-cases and not no-procedure-devised, and here the procedure was devised by the source rather than by this project. Nothing stands in the way of running it: the down is cheap, the observation needs no instrument, and the criterion is binary. What has not been done is the running."@en ;
    skos:note "What such a run would and would not settle. It would settle whether a person can exhale below the threshold at which a goose down moves, which is a question about airflow and not about the qi; the chapter treats the two as one, and this protocol does not. It would not touch the second and larger claim of the same passage, the embryonic breathing that uses neither nose nor mouth, for which the down criterion is satisfied by construction and therefore says nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "The scale beside the sign belongs in any run. The chapter starts the beginner at a silent count of one hundred and twenty and says the count may in the long run reach a thousand, and it attaches to the thousand a claim that does not belong to the down at all: that the old grow younger and a day is given back for a day. A protocol that measured the count and reported the second claim as tested would be measuring one thing and reporting another."@en .
